#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/id #uuid "d1946905-5833-58c2-9a0a-4dfd04eb922b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8963" :question/problem "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "047ea3c1-8ebf-5e26-9f28-c5a87e7cf997" :question/math-academy-id "e-8965" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147334 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "b5189ec2-ea1c-5036-9543-b963bd072ab7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9069" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n\n- $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n\n- $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = e^x y^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = -xy^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95af464f-2ceb-5d1f-91dc-a0e739b124cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144017} {:answer/id #uuid "c5078068-9c21-57a9-b473-743568265bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144018} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7f7438-80ad-5b11-a14a-9dfccd12a646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144019} {:answer/id #uuid "019412bf-e5f1-502d-b0a1-434be097c290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144020} {:answer/id #uuid "14ec42ab-455a-524b-a1e9-21d5454eff03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95af464f-2ceb-5d1f-91dc-a0e739b124cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144017} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f65c53-793c-52f3-b8f5-55b470825532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1480f672-8091-5903-a81e-b755f04694ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-106069" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x{y}^{4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{-3}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf424601-2419-5f27-8b90-c7eba796ebd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144023} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a1e672-cf1e-57d8-931b-78f5611dd482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144024} {:answer/id #uuid "120ae745-1855-5fff-97b4-5b9ad394a3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144025} {:answer/id #uuid "4682d7d3-fb20-5ef5-8d0d-69b9ce03ffd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144026} {:answer/id #uuid "77d4c96e-b5b5-54b2-a1bd-570ec2a8ae9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d4c96e-b5b5-54b2-a1bd-570ec2a8ae9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144027} :answer-field/id #uuid "b599fce9-e71e-5ffd-a98d-21b093ad0a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b36fbf4d-bb56-53ea-bcd3-bd3fbb7b094e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106070" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}=x\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/2}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2x}\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e7eb463-209e-5803-8685-e5a512aeba24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321501} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3820dc-6520-57a7-9280-7d5fefc81d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321502} {:answer/id #uuid "7230d4ca-3e31-58e4-a7bf-3b41dbfc0d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321503} {:answer/id #uuid "9222a794-6bbc-523f-bd20-00c12a22dc00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321504} {:answer/id #uuid "c6dfe1ff-a514-5308-95c8-2bf5dcd15898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e7eb463-209e-5803-8685-e5a512aeba24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321501} :answer-field/id #uuid "c54109d3-e3be-5884-906e-34b15ffdbf65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81e61808-2e93-5c92-87e6-c51f5a422b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-106071" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2})=0\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy & =3{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}y & =3{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=3{x}^{-2},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-2}=3{x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z=3{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4{x}^{-1}z=-6{x}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-6{x}^{-2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca5db28-0418-5937-9e00-d2e889cf12ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2xe^{x}}{C - 2e^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144029} {:answer/id #uuid "d39baa47-f3dd-5b18-bff0-697818f50e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{e^{x}}{C - 2e^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144030} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a6d5c3-6a20-5e40-98a7-3fe6544e043a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2e^{x}}{C - e^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144031} {:answer/id #uuid "2de561a5-72b6-5a9a-a405-d798b9fe5a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{C - e^{2x}}{2e^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144032} {:answer/id #uuid "75d8d6cf-b1b9-51d7-bbd0-f3146296a49c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{C - e^{x}}{2e^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3a6d5c3-6a20-5e40-98a7-3fe6544e043a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2e^{x}}{C - e^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144031} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d65a133-9570-530a-875f-040716d6574c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad742d4e-8bb3-5b2d-a572-286f9bbe2b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106073" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430d25f3-6c08-5505-b26a-9430a1fc60fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321508} {:answer/id #uuid "04d0872c-5646-5ec3-8d76-99eefd9c1403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321509} {:answer/id #uuid "ea721292-9638-54e7-985d-8535ce8d1d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321510} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0bbbc9-9204-566f-a231-d62890d29041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321511} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ec6cb2-5bf4-5dde-862d-6174a072c9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0bbbc9-9204-566f-a231-d62890d29041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321511} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e6d8ae-835a-5e44-8ce5-c6e1b23f6d26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5065c71-e9f3-5dc3-a977-b3142c1564e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-108911" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}$ $$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={y}^{6}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,n=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da5e6a4d-2f1b-5a20-add2-fc4099ec61dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321515} {:answer/id #uuid "60d941f7-1f89-5873-8892-fe050a1f0488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321516} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1bbe9e-b079-52a2-8902-daf72c9392b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321517} {:answer/id #uuid "f113985d-f65e-582a-9884-207ac27f7767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321518} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb5252b-dfeb-5b63-910b-338a3e51c6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb5252b-dfeb-5b63-910b-338a3e51c6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321519} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9584848-1801-5354-bc4b-49fba689114b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68ed3341-b50d-54ad-bfb9-a488c4f940ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-108913" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}y={x}^{2}{y}^{5}$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-x{y}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-{e}^{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6905ad05-d767-5812-b2d5-cf584ad2c9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321522} {:answer/id #uuid "89e17a45-db1c-5651-adaf-48b556c97013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321523} {:answer/id #uuid "38541cf7-518f-594f-abd0-73d948b4584c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321524} {:answer/id #uuid "05b91f6e-5ea2-5fcc-b287-062019742750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321525} {:answer/id #uuid "c8420ab7-fa75-54fa-b7a7-8035696e697c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8420ab7-fa75-54fa-b7a7-8035696e697c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321526} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c93ccf-a7d0-52e1-a88d-adc3b883208c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5827e29-bd33-5f0e-a7fa-1b43ad635551" :question/math-academy-id "q-108914" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=x{y}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b3c9c6e-a445-55f2-88b7-a62246545b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321529} {:answer/id #uuid "627d0d77-55b8-5be1-bdd9-c573d76d71ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321530} {:answer/id #uuid "81831e6b-d247-5d48-8642-a1a595cfde26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321531} {:answer/id #uuid "096ccb5b-e0cb-588c-ac79-3a238d2f99c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321532} {:answer/id #uuid "a993f0f5-05bc-5fa5-816c-872fc6751674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "627d0d77-55b8-5be1-bdd9-c573d76d71ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321530} :answer-field/id #uuid "fecd3a4d-7d84-56ee-987f-cdaa8eaa3628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b849caa5-cdf1-5e8c-9248-6bcaf7e0f549" :question/math-academy-id "q-108916" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}={y}^{4}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{3}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8cb3f35-11d1-59fd-bab3-f624dad92f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321536} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9e8f08-78ad-5798-9478-d0ac1981cb43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321537} {:answer/id #uuid "b457a82e-67b3-5fc3-a12b-ae3bdf0fd844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321538} {:answer/id #uuid "e34c8c0b-b3e9-5916-b6ab-09ad81baaa3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321539} {:answer/id #uuid "025cf3b0-67a6-537c-9ed6-899fb7d59c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "025cf3b0-67a6-537c-9ed6-899fb7d59c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321540} :answer-field/id #uuid "84220b9f-402b-5cda-9db8-aac0dc695f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8de450ab-0663-5275-b98e-533b8807111a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108918" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$\n- $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y})=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f884247f-c187-5c62-abff-c8b2a433e102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321543} {:answer/id #uuid "0a938b54-7bb4-5122-b4f3-af821a595a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbeaa9c-5c50-5fc0-804a-5e5aa4838165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321545} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7d0b71-b4fb-5475-ae0d-2063b12cd78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321546} {:answer/id #uuid "9481bf6d-a8c2-58a6-8013-bbbc717e164e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f7d0b71-b4fb-5475-ae0d-2063b12cd78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321546} :answer-field/id #uuid "9406f410-f316-5318-ba6d-d3b4b7823619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "116a868f-3341-5158-80f2-0367b6d92068" :question/math-academy-id "q-109009" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x={y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,n=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{5},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-5}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-4{y}^{-5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-5}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec9ebd43-6d7d-5d17-885f-3f08b160bed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321550} {:answer/id #uuid "60f672be-59eb-5b38-b7e5-85ac921a523c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321551} {:answer/id #uuid "d928e2ae-ed12-5d9e-af9a-d31b01f55957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321552} {:answer/id #uuid "38405260-ab6f-51a0-b17d-f56c264e1119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321553} {:answer/id #uuid "03bc8c21-bc42-5449-8f50-19177bb9c2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60f672be-59eb-5b38-b7e5-85ac921a523c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321551} :answer-field/id #uuid "31d4b6eb-fe21-5eb6-8c7c-d4e95278152b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd8094aa-e7b0-59c9-acb1-1eafca183add" :question/math-academy-id "q-109014" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+3xy=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3x,\\,q(x)=-{x}^{4},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+3x{y}^{-2}=-{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3xz=-{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-6xz=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-6x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26249874-c894-5357-979d-b24370fb7c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321557} {:answer/id #uuid "27173a27-dbca-5684-9713-abfd2571ef62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321558} {:answer/id #uuid "e88af2c4-817c-578f-8a48-7c81e49fd450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+C{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321559} {:answer/id #uuid "20e427ab-888f-5588-8561-75a9cd8ddf51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321560} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ecef40-f699-5a5d-b6ec-c3854183b138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}{C+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e427ab-888f-5588-8561-75a9cd8ddf51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321560} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c6a6a11-b932-534c-b176-0d494348c1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6dc285ec-042e-5dd7-8bf3-c6ac544a9a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-109026" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=x{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=x,\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+x{y}^{-1}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+xz=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-x,\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (-x) \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}) & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e8b2dd-3236-5ef1-abae-906e9c812973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321564} {:answer/id #uuid "33dee3e6-1fd5-5d3d-8770-11c9d79eda96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321565} {:answer/id #uuid "58d1df92-1703-579d-8d17-7ee95aee5015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C+2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321566} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f266e6-06f4-5beb-9273-1dd7c3416602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321567} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac158c7-8037-5a04-ba89-9fa9f2e96f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{C-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33dee3e6-1fd5-5d3d-8770-11c9d79eda96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321565} :answer-field/id #uuid "020c3836-2be9-5894-a776-42d19c589e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cbfb9b8-f301-5858-97fc-1b90fa72d9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-109158" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c223b186-25ed-5556-ae81-832fa38010f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{Cx+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321571} {:answer/id #uuid "053ed39b-2844-5c61-bdf2-11e333f54a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{C-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321572} {:answer/id #uuid "ee335274-7218-5d42-9249-2944d9b71a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321573} {:answer/id #uuid "e1dc7e47-2cd2-5189-a166-6ef6c72fdeaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{C-x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321574} {:answer/id #uuid "90c0c870-61ea-52b1-9b3f-cb0f8666d6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x+C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee335274-7218-5d42-9249-2944d9b71a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321573} :answer-field/id #uuid "04defa10-b4f3-5f01-bed1-9311fd9923c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "feae7a15-8f8f-5200-a9c1-501431f820f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109172" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-1}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z=-{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & =-x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca6a71f1-f338-56a2-ba9d-2839cbc1a566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{x}+C{x}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321578} {:answer/id #uuid "ab973e4e-44e4-5930-8f08-e443873c7255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3+Cx)}^{2/3}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321579} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb05e70-09c7-511c-a3da-b228de724fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+C}{x})}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321580} {:answer/id #uuid "6802132b-9c64-5e97-b9c3-807808dee09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2/3}-Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321581} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0fe20f-d490-5e96-8fe8-13198ca13a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a0fe20f-d490-5e96-8fe8-13198ca13a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321582} :answer-field/id #uuid "09eeedea-68f8-5e46-9bc9-875227450c10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a34e4e3f-41a3-551c-82a5-ec9f10547dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-109177" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/2x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{3/2}z & =3{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}\\,.\n$$"}]}
